[2026-03-15T09:20:44.945Z] {"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01EuLtmvkhpM8QEk5XscnYZP","type":"tool_result","content":"# Replay MCP Overview\n\nReplay MCP is a tool for inspecting Replay.io recordings, which capture complete browser execution histories. According to the documentation, it enables investigators to \"explore and investigate the app's behavior as if you are a detective with a time machine.\"\n\n## Key Capabilities\n\nThe tool provides three main categories of functionality:\n\n**Exploratory Tools** identify what happened and when, including error detection, timeline events, console messages, network requests, and React renders.\n\n**Explanatory Tools** clarify why events occurred, using stack traces, control dependencies, and state inspection at specific execution points.\n\n**Best Practices**\n\nThe documentation emphasizes a structured investigation approach: frame questions clearly, use exploratory tools to locate relevant events, employ explanatory tools to understand causation, form hypotheses supported by data, and communicate conclusions with evidence references.\n\nA particularly useful technique involves the Logpoint tool, which \"Show[s] the points and values of an expression every time a statement executed,\" helping identify unexpected behavior patterns."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a7364a07-0623-40ea-bf2b-2159671725a0","uuid":"84414a8f-4889-4460-8cc5-bb8c61266c81","tool_use_result":{"bytes":4555,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# Replay MCP Overview\n\nReplay MCP is a tool for inspecting Replay.io recordings, which capture complete browser execution histories. According to the documentation, it enables investigators to \"explore and investigate the app's behavior as if you are a detective with a time machine.\"\n\n## Key Capabilities\n\nThe tool provides three main categories of functionality:\n\n**Exploratory Tools** identify what happened and when, including error detection, timeline events, console messages, network requests, and React renders.\n\n**Explanatory Tools** clarify why events occurred, using stack traces, control dependencies, and state inspection at specific execution points.\n\n**Best Practices**\n\nThe documentation emphasizes a structured investigation approach: frame questions clearly, use exploratory tools to locate relevant events, employ explanatory tools to understand causation, form hypotheses supported by data, and communicate conclusions with evidence references.\n\nA particularly useful technique involves the Logpoint tool, which \"Show[s] the points and values of an expression every time a statement executed,\" helping identify unexpected behavior patterns.","durationMs":2880,"url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/replayio/skills/refs/heads/main/skills/replay-mcp/SKILL.md"}}
[2026-03-15T09:20:44.951Z] {"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DYr2MA95Yi4wbQEHi5fP46","type":"tool_result","content":"# Replay CLI Documentation\n\nBased on the provided documentation, here's a summary of the Replay CLI tool:\n\n## Overview\nReplay CLI allows you to record application sessions for performance analysis and debugging, then upload and manage those recordings.\n\n## Installation\nInstall globally via npm, yarn, pnpm, or bun:\n```sh\nnpm i -g replayio\n```\n\n## Key Commands\n\n**Authentication:** Set your `REPLAY_API_KEY` environment variable after creating one in your Replay.io settings.\n\n**Recording:** Launch the Replay Browser with `replayio record [url]` to capture a session.\n\n**Management:** \n- `replayio list` - View all local recordings\n- `replayio upload [ids...]` - Upload recordings to Replay\n- `replayio remove [ids...]` - Delete local recordings\n\n**Account:** Use `replayio login` or `replayio logout` for account management.\n\n## Core Workflow\n1. Record a session: `replayio record https://myapp.com`\n2. Upload when ready: `replayio upload <recording-id>`\n3. Access your recording URL after successful upload\n\nFor detailed command options and additional features like source map uploading, you can run `replayio help` or pass `-h` to any command."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a7364a07-0623-40ea-bf2b-2159671725a0","uuid":"95042ede-9a75-4d88-9cf7-3762bb9366e2","tool_use_result":{"bytes":4854,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# Replay CLI Documentation\n\nBased on the provided documentation, here's a summary of the Replay CLI tool:\n\n## Overview\nReplay CLI allows you to record application sessions for performance analysis and debugging, then upload and manage those recordings.\n\n## Installation\nInstall globally via npm, yarn, pnpm, or bun:\n```sh\nnpm i -g replayio\n```\n\n## Key Commands\n\n**Authentication:** Set your `REPLAY_API_KEY` environment variable after creating one in your Replay.io settings.\n\n**Recording:** Launch the Replay Browser with `replayio record [url]` to capture a session.\n\n**Management:** \n- `replayio list` - View all local recordings\n- `replayio upload [ids...]` - Upload recordings to Replay\n- `replayio remove [ids...]` - Delete local recordings\n\n**Account:** Use `replayio login` or `replayio logout` for account management.\n\n## Core Workflow\n1. Record a session: `replayio record https://myapp.com`\n2. Upload when ready: `replayio upload <recording-id>`\n3. Access your recording URL after successful upload\n\nFor detailed command options and additional features like source map uploading, you can run `replayio help` or pass `-h` to any command.","durationMs":3377,"url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/replayio/skills/refs/heads/main/skills/replay-cli/SKILL.md"}}
